WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.507
- Oh look who it is.

00:00:02.507 --> 00:00:03.340
Andrew!

00:00:04.448 --> 00:00:06.082
Andrew!

00:00:06.082 --> 00:00:08.159
- How did you find me?

00:00:08.159 --> 00:00:09.121
- It is the day.

00:00:09.121 --> 00:00:10.698
The day we've all been waiting for.

00:00:10.698 --> 00:00:11.531
Fried chicken.

00:00:11.531 --> 00:00:13.322
- Fried chicken.
- Fried chicken.

00:00:13.322 --> 00:00:14.363
- Let's eat that fried chicken.

00:00:14.363 --> 00:00:16.722
- Alrighty.
- Alrighty.

00:00:16.722 --> 00:00:18.049
- Today, okay.

00:00:18.049 --> 00:00:20.463
We are in New York City, baby.

00:00:20.463 --> 00:00:22.560
Today on worth it, we
are going to be trying

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:24.800
three fried chicken spots
at three drastically

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:26.334
different price points to find out which

00:00:26.334 --> 00:00:28.519
fried chicken is the most
worth it at its price.

00:00:28.519 --> 00:00:29.436
- Let's go.

00:00:30.401 --> 00:00:32.484
- We gotta talk about it.

00:00:35.114 --> 00:00:36.191
- My name is Kenneth Woods.

00:00:36.191 --> 00:00:38.448
I'm president and CEO of Sylvia's.

00:00:38.448 --> 00:00:40.425
I'm a son of Sylvia, as a matter of fact.

00:00:40.425 --> 00:00:42.533
- It's an iconic place, this restaurant.

00:00:42.533 --> 00:00:44.698
- Yeah, back in the 60's, everyone was

00:00:44.698 --> 00:00:46.811
leaving the South and coming to the city.

00:00:46.811 --> 00:00:48.459
Sylvia's is home away from home.

00:00:48.459 --> 00:00:49.932
Everyone felt comfortable.

00:00:49.932 --> 00:00:52.822
The stars, the politicians,
the mom and pop.

00:00:52.822 --> 00:00:55.673
A few weeks ago we had three birthdays.

00:00:55.673 --> 00:00:58.675
Ladies that was all over 90 years old,

00:00:58.675 --> 00:01:00.869
and three different parties.

00:01:00.869 --> 00:01:01.924
- The great equalizer.

00:01:01.924 --> 00:01:04.427
- We do it traditional Southern style.

00:01:04.427 --> 00:01:07.138
Just like how my grandma
used to make her chicken.

00:01:07.138 --> 00:01:09.436
- Is that where the recipe
comes from, is a family recipe?

00:01:09.436 --> 00:01:11.451
- Yeah. We use a three
and a half pound chicken,

00:01:11.451 --> 00:01:14.220
trim it good, then season it evenly,

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:16.403
don't just season it and
throw it in the fryer.

00:01:16.403 --> 00:01:17.911
Massage it like when you clean a

00:01:17.911 --> 00:01:20.014
baby with the baby oil on them.

00:01:20.014 --> 00:01:21.244
That's how you do the chicken.

00:01:21.244 --> 00:01:22.182
- You create a baby with love and

00:01:22.182 --> 00:01:23.259
you create a fried chicken with love.

00:01:23.259 --> 00:01:24.453
- That's right, you treat
the fried chicken with love.

00:01:24.453 --> 00:01:27.599
Let it marinate, then drench
it in all-purpose flour.

00:01:27.599 --> 00:01:30.205
Pat it to knock off the excess, fry it

00:01:30.205 --> 00:01:32.677
for 12 minutes and
enjoy what you're doing.

00:01:32.677 --> 00:01:33.510
- Yeah.

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:34.877
- It's amazing to be the oldest,

00:01:34.877 --> 00:01:36.943
the authentic, the original,

00:01:36.943 --> 00:01:40.243
all of those little phrases,
you know, mean something.

00:01:40.243 --> 00:01:42.617
- Alright, well I think its
time to try the fried chicken.

00:01:42.617 --> 00:01:44.349
- Fried freaking chicken.

00:01:44.349 --> 00:01:47.501
- One of the best meats with
one of the best preparations.

00:01:47.501 --> 00:01:49.418
Welcome to flavor town.

00:01:53.748 --> 00:01:54.787
Oh man.

00:01:54.787 --> 00:01:58.130
Oh, there's like a extra
crunchy little bit right here.

00:01:58.130 --> 00:02:00.336
- The skin is a perfect shell.

00:02:00.336 --> 00:02:03.425
- It's like a suit of
armor for a chicken knight.

00:02:03.425 --> 00:02:04.258
- You ready?

00:02:04.258 --> 00:02:05.091
- I am ready.

00:02:05.091 --> 00:02:06.284
- Oh my gosh, it's so juicy on the back.

00:02:06.284 --> 00:02:07.417
- I'm ready.

00:02:07.417 --> 00:02:09.000
- To fried chicken.

00:02:12.804 --> 00:02:13.637
Mmmm.

00:02:13.637 --> 00:02:14.715
- That's (bleep) good.

00:02:14.715 --> 00:02:15.548
- Oh I'm lost.

00:02:15.548 --> 00:02:19.578
Oh wait no, I'm in fried chicken heaven.

00:02:19.578 --> 00:02:21.873
That is like music to my ears.

00:02:21.873 --> 00:02:23.602
It's so juicy inside.

00:02:23.602 --> 00:02:25.633
It's just melting like butter.

00:02:25.633 --> 00:02:27.240
- It's a food that takes a bath

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:29.210
when it gets cooked, it has to be good.

00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:31.344
I'm shocked at how simple the preparation,

00:02:31.344 --> 00:02:32.952
actually, is to get this product.

00:02:32.952 --> 00:02:34.403
- That's when you know
you've mastered a craft,

00:02:34.403 --> 00:02:35.443
when you can make it look easy.

00:02:35.443 --> 00:02:37.074
- It really is seasoned down to the bone.

00:02:37.074 --> 00:02:39.899
Maybe the saddest sound
at a fried chicken dinner.

00:02:39.899 --> 00:02:41.628
(bone clinking on plate)

00:02:41.628 --> 00:02:43.441
That was less dramatic
than I thought it would be.

00:02:43.441 --> 00:02:44.625
- Dramatic or drumatic?

00:02:44.625 --> 00:02:45.504
- Did you just make a pun?

00:02:45.504 --> 00:02:47.752
- Dra-matic or dru-matic?

00:02:47.752 --> 00:02:49.378
- I think Steven just
made a really good pun.

00:02:49.378 --> 00:02:51.550
- Yeah that was on purpose.

00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:55.112
Let's try some of the collard greens.

00:02:55.112 --> 00:02:58.492
- That is the perfect thing
to eat alongside of this.

00:02:58.492 --> 00:03:00.263
Pretty (bleep) good right?

00:03:00.263 --> 00:03:02.132
- Just walked out of Sylvia's,
which is right there.

00:03:02.132 --> 00:03:04.253
We're going to Red Rooster,
which is over there.

00:03:04.253 --> 00:03:06.874
But, we need to walk off
some off this fried chicken.

00:03:06.874 --> 00:03:09.218
So, wanna talk a lap around the block?

00:03:09.218 --> 00:03:10.193
What did you think about Sylvia's?

00:03:10.193 --> 00:03:12.491
- Crispy, simple, juicy.

00:03:12.491 --> 00:03:13.635
Can't ask for more than that.

00:03:13.635 --> 00:03:14.810
- So now we're going to...

00:03:14.810 --> 00:03:16.368
- Wait, wait, wait, do you hear that?

00:03:16.368 --> 00:03:17.870
That's the sound of: fried chicken fact.

00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:20.003
According to the National Chicken Council,

00:03:20.003 --> 00:03:22.477
the average American eats over 90 pounds

00:03:22.477 --> 00:03:24.502
of chicken each year.

00:03:24.502 --> 00:03:25.335
- I guess that makes sense.

00:03:25.335 --> 00:03:26.969
It's gross when you think about it.

00:03:26.969 --> 00:03:29.465
Just picturing a 90 pound chicken

00:03:29.465 --> 00:03:31.689
that looks like a rottweiler.

00:03:31.689 --> 00:03:32.522
- Ew.

00:03:32.522 --> 00:03:33.526
- I guess that's like probably what a

00:03:33.526 --> 00:03:34.776
small dinosaur would look like.

00:03:34.776 --> 00:03:35.636
- Your tummy feeling okay?

00:03:35.636 --> 00:03:37.726
- It's ready for more,
it's always ready for more.

00:03:37.726 --> 00:03:38.559
- Alrighty.

00:03:38.559 --> 00:03:39.583
- Did you just try to hold my hand?

00:03:39.583 --> 00:03:40.416
- What, no.

00:03:40.416 --> 00:03:42.521
You're standing very close to me.

00:03:42.521 --> 00:03:43.465
Okay.

00:03:43.465 --> 00:03:44.798
- Fried chicken.

00:03:49.841 --> 00:03:50.674
- Welcome everybody.

00:03:50.674 --> 00:03:51.943
My name is Chef Marcus Samuelsson.

00:03:51.943 --> 00:03:53.522
Right now, you're at Red Rooster.

00:03:53.522 --> 00:03:55.139
When I thought about Red Rooster,

00:03:55.139 --> 00:03:57.369
it was about, "How do I
tackle fried chicken?"

00:03:57.369 --> 00:03:59.099
Being neighbor with the most iconic

00:03:59.099 --> 00:04:01.194
restaurant in Harlem
of all time, Sylvia's,

00:04:01.194 --> 00:04:02.826
we can talk a lot about
what we have it common.

00:04:02.826 --> 00:04:03.701
What does it mean to be a

00:04:03.701 --> 00:04:05.758
restaurant in a community like Harlem?

00:04:05.758 --> 00:04:07.349
For me, it's all about hiring.

00:04:07.349 --> 00:04:10.853
We have about 180
employees, 70% from Harlem

00:04:10.853 --> 00:04:12.870
and so I could never have dreamt that the

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:14.207
community would take so much
ownership of the restaurant.

00:04:14.207 --> 00:04:15.375
It's a push and pull.

00:04:15.375 --> 00:04:17.093
When we make a mistake,
they'll let us know,

00:04:17.093 --> 00:04:18.893
when we improve something,
they'll let us know.

00:04:18.893 --> 00:04:20.919
- What is the name of the
dish we're having today?

00:04:20.919 --> 00:04:21.799
- Fried Yard Bird.

00:04:21.799 --> 00:04:23.438
Yard Bird to me was
just like birds running

00:04:23.438 --> 00:04:24.575
around in the back, you know,

00:04:24.575 --> 00:04:25.960
when you didn't have
food, you could go out

00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:27.214
into the yard and just cut a chicken.

00:04:27.214 --> 00:04:28.775
And it's something that both from my

00:04:28.775 --> 00:04:30.326
Ethiopian and my Swedish heritage.

00:04:30.326 --> 00:04:32.166
I want a seasoned oil, so I'm flavoring

00:04:32.166 --> 00:04:34.464
it with a little bit of
rosemary, and garlic.

00:04:34.464 --> 00:04:35.297
We have our bird.

00:04:35.297 --> 00:04:37.029
A little bit of pickling
brine, sugar, salt,

00:04:37.029 --> 00:04:37.862
little bit of buttermilk.

00:04:37.862 --> 00:04:40.276
Best thing is that the bird
can be there overnight.

00:04:40.276 --> 00:04:42.255
And then in our flour
mix, we have a shake,

00:04:42.255 --> 00:04:44.334
but it's the one ingredient that I will

00:04:44.334 --> 00:04:45.809
let you know that's in that mix.

00:04:45.809 --> 00:04:46.642
- And what is that?

00:04:46.642 --> 00:04:47.965
- That is a spice blend from Ethiopia.

00:04:47.965 --> 00:04:50.135
Large chilies, not super spiced ginger,

00:04:50.135 --> 00:04:53.535
garlic, it has this
beautiful taste of Ethiopia.

00:04:53.535 --> 00:04:55.933
You want the right amount of flour,

00:04:55.933 --> 00:04:57.374
so you get that crunchiness.

00:04:57.374 --> 00:04:59.038
I landed on twice fried chicken.

00:04:59.038 --> 00:05:01.606
The first fry is really all
about cooking it through.

00:05:01.606 --> 00:05:03.040
Then you lift it out, let it rest.

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:04.831
Then you just dip it again and fry it

00:05:04.831 --> 00:05:06.493
to get really nice and crunchy.

00:05:06.493 --> 00:05:07.902
I have a little bit of oil from that

00:05:07.902 --> 00:05:09.278
flavored oil that we cooked in,

00:05:09.278 --> 00:05:11.996
little bit of honey,
and our Rooster sauce.

00:05:11.996 --> 00:05:13.950
All of that stuff that
I've used, comes back in.

00:05:13.950 --> 00:05:15.768
It's really a reflection
of poor man's cooking.

00:05:15.768 --> 00:05:17.157
If you ever think about
the food of the South,

00:05:17.157 --> 00:05:18.368
everyone that was working class

00:05:18.368 --> 00:05:19.572
ate pretty much the same.

00:05:19.572 --> 00:05:21.662
Out of that came certain flavor points.

00:05:21.662 --> 00:05:25.056
As the migration moved the
population up, the food stayed.

00:05:25.056 --> 00:05:26.786
Obviously, it tastes
different in New York or

00:05:26.786 --> 00:05:30.368
Detroit than it does in
Virginia or Kentucky.

00:05:30.368 --> 00:05:33.299
The methods stay just
as much as jazz came up,

00:05:33.299 --> 00:05:35.047
and today we have hip hop because of it.

00:05:35.047 --> 00:05:37.747
The DNA is really the
foods of the migration.

00:05:37.747 --> 00:05:38.706
- So we got the Yard Bird.

00:05:38.706 --> 00:05:40.564
- We also got some bourbon on the rocks,

00:05:40.564 --> 00:05:43.616
because it's a hot day in Harlem.

00:05:43.616 --> 00:05:44.639
- Oh I'm ready now.

00:05:44.639 --> 00:05:46.482
Strangely, very sweet.

00:05:46.482 --> 00:05:48.128
- Yeah, he said there's
a hot honey in that.

00:05:48.128 --> 00:05:50.537
Hot honey is what they
called me in high school.

00:05:50.537 --> 00:05:51.817
- Tell me who called you that.

00:05:51.817 --> 00:05:52.725
- No one.

00:05:52.725 --> 00:05:54.977
- Dibs on that one.

00:05:54.977 --> 00:05:55.993
- Sure, go for it.

00:05:55.993 --> 00:05:57.791
Because this one's clearly better.

00:05:57.791 --> 00:05:58.711
- Oooh.

00:05:58.711 --> 00:06:00.128
- Smells so good.

00:06:01.371 --> 00:06:02.204
Oh my god.

00:06:03.119 --> 00:06:04.218
- I got it on my nose.

00:06:04.218 --> 00:06:05.468
Chicken cheers.

00:06:10.223 --> 00:06:14.432
- That is some juicy
chicken, holy (bleep).

00:06:14.432 --> 00:06:16.706
- That is a sophisticated flavor.

00:06:16.706 --> 00:06:19.824
- It's that nice, deep, roasted, slightly

00:06:19.824 --> 00:06:22.088
bitter nutty flavor that
he was talking about.

00:06:22.088 --> 00:06:23.840
- Andrew with the adjectives.

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:25.423
- Adjective Andrew.

00:06:26.872 --> 00:06:27.760
- Ooh.

00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:30.225
- I can't believe how juicy this meat is.

00:06:30.225 --> 00:06:31.393
And the skin is...

00:06:31.393 --> 00:06:32.226
- Perfection.
- Oh, (bleep).

00:06:32.226 --> 00:06:33.550
- I just got some skin in my Bourbon.

00:06:33.550 --> 00:06:36.662
- The skin is crispy and
it melts in your mouth.

00:06:36.662 --> 00:06:37.732
That's like making love

00:06:37.732 --> 00:06:40.893
and I don't have any
experience in that department.

00:06:40.893 --> 00:06:41.726
- Really?

00:06:41.726 --> 00:06:43.334
- I don't, but if I'm
gonna be honest here,

00:06:43.334 --> 00:06:44.399
that's what I imagine it's like.

00:06:44.399 --> 00:06:45.926
The chicken and I have become one.

00:06:45.926 --> 00:06:48.808
- You know what, I'm not
gonna spoil anything for you.

00:06:48.808 --> 00:06:50.708
I got it all over my face, I know.

00:06:50.708 --> 00:06:52.390
- [Cameraman] You look like the Joker.

00:06:52.390 --> 00:06:54.325
It's so good.
- It's crazy.

00:06:54.325 --> 00:06:55.982
- Can I jump in?
- Please.

00:06:55.982 --> 00:06:57.399
- I really thought I was
gonna get to eat that,

00:06:57.399 --> 00:06:58.246
but it's okay.

00:06:58.246 --> 00:07:01.120
- Should we go to the
cornbread next to soak this up?

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:02.186
- It's grimy, man.

00:07:02.186 --> 00:07:03.019
- Grimy in a good way?

00:07:03.019 --> 00:07:04.227
- (Chef Marcus) Yeah, word to the bird.

00:07:04.227 --> 00:07:05.404
And like that, he's gone.

00:07:05.404 --> 00:07:06.848
The structural integrity of this

00:07:06.848 --> 00:07:09.680
skin with the coating is unreal.

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:13.560
It's like soft and supple,
but strong and mysterious.

00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:15.621
- Adjective Andrew, back at it again.

00:07:15.621 --> 00:07:17.984
- Adjective Andrew.

00:07:17.984 --> 00:07:19.484
Sweet potato yams.

00:07:20.776 --> 00:07:23.377
The yams are like dessert mashed potatoes.

00:07:23.377 --> 00:07:24.867
So there's one piece of chicken left.

00:07:24.867 --> 00:07:26.034
Can I have it?

00:07:30.200 --> 00:07:31.173
- Can we order some more?

00:07:31.173 --> 00:07:34.237
- I asked Marcus if he had
any suggestions for dessert.

00:07:34.237 --> 00:07:35.875
- It's so hot.

00:07:35.875 --> 00:07:38.936
- Turns out, Make My Cake,
makes some great cake.

00:07:38.936 --> 00:07:39.769
Thank you.

00:07:42.519 --> 00:07:43.662
That may have been...

00:07:43.662 --> 00:07:45.289
- It was the best fried
chicken I've ever had.

00:07:45.289 --> 00:07:46.122
I said it first.

00:07:46.122 --> 00:07:48.161
- I couldn't have said it better...

00:07:48.161 --> 00:07:50.635
- We went to the quintessential
Harlem fried chicken spot.

00:07:50.635 --> 00:07:53.283
- Now we're going to:
fried chicken fact town.

00:07:53.283 --> 00:07:54.525
- Oh, I forgot about that.

00:07:54.525 --> 00:07:56.322
- How much chicken do you think the

00:07:56.322 --> 00:07:57.953
United States consumes every year?

00:07:57.953 --> 00:07:59.393
- A trillion chickens.

00:07:59.393 --> 00:08:00.914
- A trillion chickens?

00:08:00.914 --> 00:08:02.144
- I'll recalculate a little bit.

00:08:02.144 --> 00:08:04.472
- Alright, recalibrate.

00:08:04.472 --> 00:08:05.529
- Two billion chickens.

00:08:05.529 --> 00:08:07.186
- Not bad, eight billion chickens.

00:08:07.186 --> 00:08:08.045
- Eight billion?

00:08:08.045 --> 00:08:10.129
- Eight billion chickens.

00:08:10.129 --> 00:08:12.744
- So we ate a billion chickens,
I was only a billion off.

00:08:12.744 --> 00:08:14.439
- No, we eat eight billion chickens.

00:08:14.439 --> 00:08:16.014
- So we just went to Sylvia's,

00:08:16.014 --> 00:08:17.678
the quintessential fried chicken spot.

00:08:17.678 --> 00:08:18.511
- Iconic.

00:08:18.511 --> 00:08:19.344
- Red Rooster.

00:08:19.344 --> 00:08:20.177
- Game changer.

00:08:20.177 --> 00:08:22.109
- Now, we're going downtown.

00:08:22.109 --> 00:08:24.559
Fried chicken will be
served at a noodle bar.

00:08:24.559 --> 00:08:28.726
It's really hot in New York,
my brain no good, no more.

00:08:32.829 --> 00:08:33.741
- Hi, I'm Dave Chang.

00:08:33.741 --> 00:08:34.574
We've met before.

00:08:34.574 --> 00:08:36.744
We're at Momofuku Noodle
Bar, in the East Village.

00:08:36.744 --> 00:08:38.567
- Last time we saw you was in Korea.

00:08:38.567 --> 00:08:40.360
- I think you should try
to eat the chili pepper.

00:08:40.360 --> 00:08:41.193
- Is it good?

00:08:41.193 --> 00:08:42.783
- Yeah, it's spicy as (bleep).

00:08:42.783 --> 00:08:43.616
- Woooo.

00:08:44.455 --> 00:08:46.759
Now, we get to try your food.

00:08:46.759 --> 00:08:48.065
Does that make you nervous?

00:08:48.065 --> 00:08:49.070
- I'm terrified.

00:08:49.070 --> 00:08:49.903
- Why?

00:08:49.903 --> 00:08:51.706
- Because I'm competitive,
and if we don't win-

00:08:51.706 --> 00:08:52.541
- It's not a competition.

00:08:52.541 --> 00:08:54.049
- I'm probably the most
competitive person.

00:08:54.049 --> 00:08:55.582
Basically, we're serving fried chicken

00:08:55.582 --> 00:08:57.285
like peking duck with caviar.

00:08:57.285 --> 00:09:00.907
I understand how stupid and
almost douche-y it sounds.

00:09:00.907 --> 00:09:02.615
By no means am I trying to disrespect

00:09:02.615 --> 00:09:04.610
fried chicken or caviar.

00:09:04.610 --> 00:09:05.927
I think it works tremendously.

00:09:05.927 --> 00:09:07.814
The person that's actually
cooking is Tony Kim.

00:09:07.814 --> 00:09:09.607
He's executive chef of
all the Noodle Bars.

00:09:09.607 --> 00:09:11.071
